<k>quinceberry</k>
<blockquote><blockquote><b>quinceberry</b></blockquote></blockquote>
<blockquote><blockquote>(<c c="darkslategray">ˈkwɪnsbɛrɪ</c>)</blockquote></blockquote>
<blockquote><blockquote><c c="gray">[f. <abr>OFr.</abr> <i>coinz</i> quince + <kref>berry</kref> n., on the</c></blockquote></blockquote>
<blockquote><blockquote><c c="gray">pattern of <i>bilberry</i>: known only from western glossaries.]</c></blockquote></blockquote>
<blockquote><blockquote>The fruit of a hedge shrub of the rose family, formerly gathered for preserves.</blockquote></blockquote>
<blockquote><blockquote><blockquote><blockquote><ex><b>1601</b> <i>J. Gerard</i> <i>Herball</i> II. iv. 12 <c c="darkmagenta">The quinceberrie is of a sharpe taste.</c> <b>1663</b> <i>Sylva Rustica</i> 44 <c c="darkmagenta">Quinceberries, being stewed, make a conserve.</c> <b>1728</b> <i>E. Thacker</i> <i>Kitchen Bk.</i> 91 <c c="darkmagenta">Take of quinceberries two pound.</c> <b>1810</b> <i>Dial. Gloss. W. Riding</i> 8 <c c="darkmagenta">Quinceberry, a hedge fruit.</c> <b>1902</b> <i>Country Life</i> 3 May 511 <c c="darkmagenta">The quinceberry is now seldom gathered.</c> <b>1994</b> <i>M. Oakes</i> <i>Hedgerow Cookery</i> 27 <c c="darkmagenta">Quinceberry jelly needs no pectin.</c></ex></blockquote></blockquote></blockquote></blockquote>
<blockquote><blockquote>Also <i>attrib.</i>, as <b>quinceberry jelly</b>.</blockquote></blockquote>
<blockquote><blockquote><blockquote><blockquote><ex><b>1890</b> <i>Gardeners’ Chron.</i> 12 Apr. 460 <c c="darkmagenta">A quinceberry hedge.</c> <b>1931</b> <i>H. Vane</i> <i>Orchard Notes</i> 18 <c c="darkmagenta">Quinceberry jelly, an old receipt.</c></ex></blockquote></blockquote></blockquote></blockquote>
